data.content:
Since `TimestampFormatter` is a `static` class, you can't use constructor injection directly. You have two main options:

### Option 1 — Make it a non-static class (recommended)
Convert to an injectable service so DI wires up `TimeProvider` naturally:

```csharp
public class TimestampFormatter(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string Now() => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

Register it in DI alongside `TimeProvider`.

### Option 2 — Accept `TimeProvider` as a parameter
Keep it static but pass the dependency explicitly:

```csharp
public static class TimestampFormatter
{
    public static string Now(TimeProvider timeProvider)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

**Option 1 is the standard .NET approach** — it's testable, idiomatic, and works naturally with DI. Want me to apply it? If so, should I also invoke the **migrate-static-to-wrapper** skill to handle updating all call sites?
